---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Timeline from '../components/Timeline.astro';
import TrackNotes from '../components/TrackNotes.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import { tracks, notesForWeek, totalWeeks, noteHref } from '../data/roadmap';
import { weeklyPractice } from '../data/practice';

const weeks = Array.from({ length: totalWeeks }, (_, i) => i + 1);
const themeOf = (week: number) => weeklyPractice.find((w) => w.week === week)?.theme ?? '';
---

<Base title="Roadmap">
  <div class="prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">The learning path</p>
    <h1>Roadmap</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Sixteen weeks, nine subject tracks, five projects. Math and classical ML first and in parallel, then deep
      learning, then everything that makes an AI engineer.
    </p>

    <Callout kind="plain">
      <p>
        An AI engineer builds products on top of language models. To do that well you need three things. You need the
        base: enough math and classical ML to read a formula and reason about data (tracks 1–2). You need to know how
        the engine works inside (tracks 3–5). And you need to know how to build a reliable car around it (tracks 6–8).
        The last track is practice at explaining all of it out loud.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout kind="note" label="The scope rule">
      <p>
        Math, statistics and machine learning are each a lifetime of study. Here, every topic has to earn its place by
        one of two tests: an AI engineer interview asks about it, or a later note needs it. Anything that passes
        neither test is left out on purpose, and the
        <a href="/notes/#math">math track</a> ends with a note listing what you may safely skip.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <Timeline />

    <h2>Why math and ML run together</h2>
    <p>
      Studying math alone is dry and studying ML without it is confusing. So each week pairs the two: the math note
      gives you the tool, and the ML note of the same week uses it immediately. Vectors and dot products appear on
      Monday, linear regression uses them on Tuesday. Derivatives arrive in week 2 and gradient descent spends them the
      same week. By week 5 the <a href="/notes/dl/what-is-a-neural-network/">neural network note</a> reads easily,
      because every symbol in it has already done a job.
    </p>

    <h2>How to study each day</h2>
    <p>
      The order matters twice over. Read the notes in the order the week-by-week table below gives, because each note
      only uses words that earlier notes explained; and meet each idea three times, in three forms, because that is
      what makes it stick. If a word stops you, hover over it (dotted underline) or open the
      <a href="/glossary/">glossary</a>; if a line of code stops you, click the <code>np.</code> call to open the
      <a href="/numpy/">NumPy reference</a>.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Watch or read the source</strong> for the topic (the lecture or chapter linked inside the note).</li>
      <li><strong>Read our note</strong> on the same topic. Redraw its main diagram on paper from memory.</li>
      <li><strong>Do the practice.</strong> Hand problems on paper first, then the coding drill typed by hand. Do not paste.</li>
      <li><strong>Say the interview answer out loud</strong> without looking. If you get stuck, that is the part to reread.</li>
      <li><strong>Answer the practice questions</strong> at the end of the note before opening them.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Day</th><th>What to do</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Monday to Friday</td><td>One or two notes a day with their practice drills. In weeks 1–4, alternate a math note and an ML note.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Saturday</td><td>Project work only. Build the milestone listed for that week on the <a href="/practice/">practice page</a>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sunday</td><td>Revision cards and practice questions for the whole week, plus a timed drill from the <a href="/practice/">practice page</a>. Then rest.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <Callout kind="note" label="No GPU needed">
      <p>
        Everything here runs on a normal laptop, on the free GPU hours from Google Colab or Kaggle, or through hosted
        model APIs that cost a few dollars in total. Each project page says which one it uses.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="week-by-week">Week by week</h2>
    <p>
      Each row is one week, and the notes are listed <strong>in the order to read them</strong>. In weeks 1–4 that order
      alternates between the ML and the math track on purpose: the ML note raises a question and the next math note
      answers it, so no note uses a word that an earlier one has not explained. The full practice for the week is on the
      <a href="/practice/">practice page</a>.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="schedule">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Week</th><th>Theme</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            weeks.map((week) => (
              <tr>
                <td>W{week}</td>
                <td>{themeOf(week)}</td>
                <td>
                  <ol class="reading-order">
                    {notesForWeek(week).map(({ track, note }) => (
                      <li>
                        <span class="badge track">{track.short}</span>
                        {note.ready ? <a href={noteHref(track, note)}>{note.title}</a> : <span class="pending">{note.title}</span>}
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ol>
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>The nine tracks</h2>
    {
      tracks.map((track) => (
        <section class="phase" id={track.id}>
          <div class="phase-head">
            <span class="phase-number">{track.number}</span>
            <h3>{track.title}</h3>
            <span class="phase-weeks">{track.weeks}</span>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>Goal.</strong> {track.goal}
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Scope.</strong> {track.scope}
          </p>
          {track.project && (
            <p>
              <strong>Build.</strong> <a href="/projects/">{track.project}</a>
            </p>
          )}
          <p>
            <strong>Study from.</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            {track.sources.map((s) => <li>{s}</li>)}
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Notes.</strong>
          </p>
          <TrackNotes track={track} />
        </section>
      ))
    }

    <h2>How you know a track is done</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>You can redraw every main diagram of the track on a blank page.</li>
      <li>You can give each note’s interview answer in under two minutes, without notes.</li>
      <li>You can do the week’s hand problems without looking anything up.</li>
      <li>You can answer at least four in five practice questions correctly.</li>
      <li>The project milestone for the track runs, and its README explains what you measured.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Base>
